<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tujibids</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Banu Tujib, Tujibid dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tujibids were an Arab family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March of al-Andalus</a>, descended from the Yemeni tribe of Tujib, who replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> as its leading lords in the 10th century. They governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daroca">Daroca</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and after the caliphate collapsed they ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of Zaragoza from 1018 until 1039, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hud">Banu Hud</a> overthrew them.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The historian Ibn Hazm traced the family, also called the Banu al-Muhajir, to two sons of al-Muhajir ibn Naywa, ʿAmira and ʿAbd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-nusayr">Musa ibn Nusayr</a> for the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the early 710s and settled in Aragon. In the second half of the 9th century Emir Muhammad I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> recruited the sons of ʿAbd al-ʿAziz ibn ʿAbd al-Rahman al-Tujibi against the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a>, granting them towns including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daroca">Daroca</a>. In 862/3 he gave the rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> to ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAziz, whose sons founded the lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, Calatayud and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In January 890 ʿAbd al-Rahman's son Muhammad gained the trust of the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, Ahmad ibn al-Barraʼ al-Qurashi, murdered him and had himself named governor, founding what was in effect an autonomous hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. He held out against a 17-year siege by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a>. His grandson Muhammad ibn Hashim allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-leon">Ramiro II of León</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> ʿAbd al-Rahman III, who took Zaragoza in 937 but restored him as a tributary; he was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas">Battle of Simancas</a> in 939 and ransomed. In 989 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> executed the Tujibid leader ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad for joining a plot by one of Almanzor's sons.</p><p>Al-Mundhir ibn Yahya, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daroca">Daroca</a> branch, who had begun as a simple soldier, became governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March</a> and in 1018 declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> independent. His grandson al-Mundhir II was murdered in 1039 by his cousin, the qadi ʿAbd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn al-Hakam al-Tujibi, whom the people saw as a usurper. ʿAbd Allah fled to Rueda with the treasury, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-ibn-hud">Sulayman ibn Hud</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lérida</a>, took Zaragoza as al-Mustaʿin I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A junior line descended from Sumadih, the Banu Sumadih, fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> after a quarrel with al-Mundhir I. Its ruler made Maʿn ibn Muhammad governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> in 1038, and in 1042 Maʿn made it an independent taifa. His son al-Muʿtasim, a noted poet, ruled for 41 years; his successor Ahmad Muʿizz al-Dawla abandoned Almería to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> and died in exile at Dellys in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banu Tujib” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later family members fought Córdoba, the <b>Tujibids</b> and Navarre.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a></li><li><blockquote>After a period under Valencia, the Arab <b>Tujibid</b> family of the Banu Sumadih took power in 1041 under Man ibn Sumadih.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-almeria">Taifa of Almería</a></li><li><blockquote>Zaragoza, capital of the Upper March, came under the Arab <b>Tujibid</b> governor Mundhir I around 1018.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-zaragoza">Taifa of Zaragoza</a></li></ul></section>
